WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.664
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:09.443
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:17.039
Unit twenty-five: Writing an Academic Essay. Today you will link ideas across sentences with however, moreover and consequently.

4
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:19.736
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.049
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:25.815
Essay.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:30.500
Essay means a piece of writing that develops an argument on one topic.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:33.594
The essay argued for stronger libraries.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.290
Your turn. Say: Essay.

10
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:39.856
Essay.

11
00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:41.583
Thesis.

12
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:45.500
Thesis means the central claim that an essay defends.

13
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:48.685
The thesis appears at the end of the introduction.

14
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:51.854
Your turn. Say: Thesis.

15
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:55.469
Thesis.

16
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:57.171
Paragraph.

17
00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:01.414
Paragraph means a group of sentences about one main idea.

18
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:04.585
Each paragraph begins with a topic sentence.

19
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:07.998
Your turn. Say: Paragraph.

20
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:11.846
Paragraph.

21
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:13.468
Evidence.

22
00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:18.201
Evidence means facts, examples or quotations that support a claim.

23
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:21.106
Strong evidence makes the argument credible.

24
00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:24.497
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

25
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:28.183
Evidence.

26
00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.207
Analysis.

27
00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:35.062
Analysis means explaining what the evidence shows and why it matters.

28
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.369
Analysis separates a good essay from a summary.

29
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:42.158
Your turn. Say: Analysis.

30
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.222
Analysis.

31
00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:48.112
Counterargument.

32
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:53.647
Counterargument means a reason that opposes the thesis and is answered in the essay.

33
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.016
The counterargument is answered in the fourth paragraph.

34
00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.153
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

35
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.233
Counterargument.

36
00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:05.996
Conclusion.

37
00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:10.283
Conclusion means the final section that sums up the argument.

38
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:13.696
The conclusion restates the thesis in new words.

39
00:02:14.283 --> 00:02:16.970
Your turn. Say: Conclusion.

40
00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:20.951
Conclusion.

41
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:22.904
Citation.

42
00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:28.099
Citation means a reference that credits the source of an idea or quotation.

43
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:31.147
Every quotation needs a citation.

44
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.553
Your turn. Say: Citation.

45
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.448
Citation.

46
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:42.640
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:49.157
Visal, an essay says 'The data are limited. However, the trend is clear.' What does 'However' do?

48
00:02:50.250 --> 00:03:03.368
It is a discourse marker. It shows how two ideas relate. However contrasts, moreover adds, consequently shows result, similarly compares. Markers guide the reader through the argument.

49
00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:05.914
How do we punctuate them?

50
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:23.346
Start a new sentence with a capital letter and put a comma after the marker: However, the trend is clear. Within a sentence, use a semicolon before it: The data are limited; however, the trend is clear.

51
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.869
Can I use too many?

52
00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:43.376
Yes. Do not begin every sentence with a marker. Use them where the logic needs signposting. Choose the right one: moreover adds a similar point; consequently gives a result, not a contrast.

53
00:03:44.533 --> 00:03:45.999
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:51.531
Grammar focus: Linking: However, and Moreover, and Consequently,.

55
00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:53.134
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.083
The data are limited. However, the trend is clear.

57
00:03:57.983 --> 00:04:01.379
The method is reliable. Moreover, it is inexpensive.

58
00:04:02.283 --> 00:04:06.282
Funding was cut. Consequently, the project ended early.

59
00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:16.097
However contrasts, moreover adds, consequently shows result. Put a comma after the marker at the start of a sentence; use a semicolon before it inside a sentence.

60
00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.861
Be careful! Don't say: Data is small, however it is clear.

61
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.984
Say: Data is small; however, it is clear.

62
00:04:26.284 --> 00:04:29.050
Use a semicolon before and a comma after however.

63
00:04:29.833 --> 00:04:32.439
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:34.594
Building an Argument.

65
00:04:34.894 --> 00:04:38.473
A strong essay begins with a thesis that makes one clear claim.

66
00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:46.354
Each paragraph develops one idea with evidence and analysis; moreover, each citation shows where the support comes from.

67
00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:52.620
A good writer includes a counterargument. However, the essay must answer it fairly.

68
00:04:53.633 --> 00:05:00.114
Consequently, the argument becomes more convincing, because the reader sees that the writer has considered other views.

69
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.864
The conclusion restates the thesis and shows why the argument matters.

70
00:05:05.967 --> 00:05:09.667
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:10.517 --> 00:05:13.445
The idea is simple; blank, it is powerful.

72
00:05:13.745 --> 00:05:17.491
A, however. B, because. C, although.

73
00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:23.103
The answer is A: however.

74
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:27.004
The central claim that an essay defends is the blank.

75
00:05:27.304 --> 00:05:31.039
A, citation. B, thesis. C, paragraph.

76
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.449
The answer is B: thesis.

77
00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:39.896
Which sentence is punctuated correctly?

78
00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:46.787
A, Moreover the result is clear.. B, Moreover, the result is clear.. C, Moreover; the result is clear..

79
00:05:50.333 --> 00:05:53.297
The answer is B: Moreover, the result is clear..

80
00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.599
A reference that credits the source of an idea is a blank.

81
00:05:57.899 --> 00:06:02.052
A, citation. B, conclusion. C, counterargument.

82
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.588
The answer is A: citation.

83
00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:12.131
According to the reading, what must an essay do with a counterargument?

84
00:06:12.431 --> 00:06:16.444
A, answer it fairly. B, ignore it. C, hide it.

85
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.336
The answer is A: answer it fairly.

86
00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:26.476
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:29.966
What is the hardest part of writing an essay for you?

88
00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.297
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.597 --> 00:07:00.309
For me, the hardest part is organising my ideas before I start. I often have many thoughts, but I need a clear thesis and a logical order. Moreover, choosing the right evidence takes time. When I make a plan first, my paragraphs connect better and the essay is much easier to write.

90
00:07:00.609 --> 00:07:02.342
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:08.747
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and linking: however, and moreover, and consequently,.

92
00:07:09.047 --> 00:07:13.139
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

93
00:07:13.439 --> 00:07:21.748
Congratulations! You have finished Online Newspaper Class, level five. See you in Online Newspaper Class, with many more new topics!

94
00:07:22.048 --> 00:07:24.921
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

